The final job is a robot that does a useful thing to a synth, safely, and that Joel can explain. Each milestone is a smaller, complete version of that job.

| Milestone | Whole task | Based on |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [M1. Bench it](/lore/m1-bench-it) | Power the board, turn one motor, stop it safely | Official docs |
| [M2. One joint end to end](/lore/m2-one-joint) | Build the wrist, check its sensor, then measure a commanded move of joint 6 | Official docs |
| [M3. The whole arm moves](/lore/m3-whole-arm) | Build the rest of the arm, wrist to base, then home, jog and check the witness marks | Official docs |
| [M4. Pick and place](/lore/m4-pick-and-place) | Fit a gripper and measure a repeated loop | Official docs |
| [M5. Touch the skiff](/lore/m5-touch-the-skiff) | Register the skiff, turn a knob, press a button | Our design |
| [M6. Patch a cable](/lore/m6-patch-a-cable) | Insert a patch cable and verify it | Our design |
| [M7. Play it](/lore/m7-play-it) | Perform a short written piece | Our design |

<Diagram alt="The seven milestones as a ladder: M1 to M4 follow the official manual, M5 to M7 are our design and go past it">

```text
  M7  Play it               ┐
  M6  Patch a cable         │ our design
  M5  Touch the skiff       ┘
  ─────────────────────────────────────
  M4  Pick and place        ┐
  M3  The whole arm moves   │ official manual
  M2  One joint end to end  │
  M1  Bench it              ┘
```

</Diagram>

## How each milestone is built

Every milestone page has the same parts:

- **Whole task:** the complete job for this stage.
- **Parts:** what goes on the bench, with links to the official [bill of materials](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/build/bill-of-materials/).
- **Concepts:** the mental model, the why.
- **How-to:** the official page for each step, opened when that step comes up.
- **Practice:** short drills for hand skills that come back in every milestone.
- **Done check:** Joel does the job cold, on camera.
- **Video:** the plan for the milestone's release, on its [video page](/lore/videos).

The structure comes from [four-component instructional design](https://www.4cid.org/). The official build step is the worked example. Our variation of it is the completion task. The done check is the conventional task, with no help.

## Honesty markers

**needs_source** marks a claim the official sources do not cover yet. **Our design** marks milestones 5 to 7, which go past the PAROL6 manual. Both stay visible until a source or a measurement settles them.

## Terms

- **milestone**: one stage of the build, with a whole task, a done check and a video.
- **whole task**: a complete, smaller version of the final job, practised from start to finish ([4C/ID](https://www.4cid.org/)).
- **task class**: a group of whole tasks at the same level of difficulty, sequenced from simple to complex ([4C/ID](https://www.4cid.org/)).
- **done check**: the milestone's final test, done cold and on camera.
